Fixed both halves of what you described, and they were the same defect seen from two sides.

**Why clicking 1 showed 1-5.** A plain row's double-click stages through `playPicked`, and its first choice is "stage the slide the *preview* is on" (`playSlideAt(previewSlideIndex)`). That index is whatever the last pick set — so after clicking 1-6, double-clicking row 1 staged 1-6's page while the list said 1. It only behaved when the previous pick had left the index on the right page — which is exactly your other observation: click 1-1 first, then 1 works, because 1-1 had already re-seated the index on paragraph 1.

**What changed, in [ReferencePane.qml](qml/components/ReferencePane.qml):**
- `playPicked` now reuses the previewed slide **only if that slide's parts belong to the current pick**; otherwise it falls through and builds the pick itself, and records the pick's own first piece as the follow target.
- The follow no longer re-derives the piece from the staged slide's `parts[0]` (the paragraph's *head*, which is what dragged the list back to the top on 1-7). When the operator's own piece is present it now *applies* that piece: selection, preview index (the slide carrying it) and the list's row. That also fixes the stale preview my previous guard had left behind — skipping the follow was not the same as correcting it.

