Done, and the budget is per source, not global — that was the key detail.

**How it's wired**

- Each tab keeps its own setting: `scripture.wordsPerSlide` (Bible), `table.wordsPerSlide` (The Table), `shows.wordsPerSlide` (Shows) — all three already declared, with their own labels/ranges, in [AppSettings.cpp](src/PresentationEngine/modules/settings/AppSettings.cpp). The reading fit reads whichever tab staged the pick, via that tab's `optionsPrefix`, so nothing is tied to 40 or to the Table.
- [LiveOutputService.h](qml/services/include/services/LiveOutputService.h) — a new `readingWordBudget` property (clamped to the setting's own 3–1000 range), so a surface can ask "what is a full screenful here?" without knowing about tabs.
- [ReferencePane.qml](qml/components/ReferencePane.qml) — `syncReadingWordBudget()` reads `<this tab>.wordsPerSlide` and hands it over at every stage: a click, the ‹ › arrows, a continuation row, a play. Change the Bible's budget and only the Bible's reading changes.
- [DesignPreview.qml](qml/components/DesignPreview.qml) — the reading block's bisection now measures a **probe of that budget's words** (the passage's first N words) instead of the page's own text; static previews still fit their own page. So every page of a passage solves to one size for a given template box, and since no page is longer than the budget, nothing can overflow.

Built clean (`DEV_CYCLE: PASS`, all three guards passed) and the app is running (pid 9404). The 40 appears only as the fallback used when no surface has reported a budget yet (the very first frame), never as the value the Table is stuck with.

Two things I have not verified myself: the visual result across a wordy page (47-0412 with Alt+Down is the case that was clipping), and whether a page you have *deliberately* filled above the budget — `splitLongItems` off, or a very long single word — still needs the old per-page squeeze as a safety net. If it does, that's a small follow-up on the same code path.
