The mode now persists per tab, keyed to whichever tab owns the wall's arrows.

## What I changed

**Per-tab keys, declared in [AppSettings.cpp](src/PresentationEngine/modules/settings/AppSettings.cpp)** — `scripture.scrollWalk`, `table.scrollWalk`, `shows.scrollWalk`, each a `Bool` defaulting to `false` (Slide). They're declared next to the shared fill-tab table so they follow the same three prefixes as `wordsPerSlide`, but no settings card lists them: the mode is shown and changed by the wall's own transport button, and the key only exists so the choice survives a restart. Declaring them matters — an undeclared key refuses every write, so the mode would silently reset each launch.

**The service now says which tab owns the arrows** — `LiveOutputService.wallStepperId` in [LiveOutputService.h](qml/services/include/services/LiveOutputService.h) / [.cpp](qml/services/LiveOutputService.cpp), set in `setWallStepper`. When the owning tab withdraws, another claiming tab takes over rather than leaving it blank, and a change of owner is signalled even while the aggregate stays lit — so a surface keying per-tab state re-reads at the right moment.

**The wall adopts and writes back** — [MonitorWall.qml](qml/components/MonitorWall.qml) derives `modeKey` from that owner (`scripture` / `shows` / `table`), adopts the stored value when the owner changes and at completion, and writes the choice back whenever `LiveOutputService.scrollMode` changes. So The Table returns in Scroll while a show returns in Slide, and the button press is the only thing that writes.

Your own edits stayed as the foundation: the service's `scrollMode` property, the pane consulting it in `stepAndPlay`, and `setReadingTextSource` activating straight away when the mode is on.

