Scroll mode now fills the box instead of dividing at paragraph edges.

## What was still wrong

Your paging was already 40-words-per-page across paragraphs — the log from your run shows the walk holding `set=325` pages with 17 and 16 lines each, i.e. pages that carry on past a paragraph end. What made it *not* read like a book was the join: `readingPagesFromChapter` stitched the chapter items together with a hard **newline**, so any page that crossed a paragraph boundary started the next paragraph on a fresh line inside the same box. That is a visible division in the middle of a page, and it is what the "we don't separate" complaint is about.

## The change

In [ReferencePane.qml](qml/components/ReferencePane.qml), the chapter text is now built as one continuous passage — items join with a single space, not `
`. Consequences:

- A page that begins at paragraph 1 keeps filling with words from paragraph 2, 3… up to the tab's max-words-per-screen, with no break where the paragraph changed.
- The only division left is the page break at the word budget, exactly like turning to the next page of a book.
- The pivot page still starts on the exact word of the clicked paragraph, and pages before it still exist, so scrolling back walks towards the start of the sermon.

On "no expand to fill": nothing in the scroll path re-fits now. The pane's own arrows return from `stepAndPlay` *before* `noteOperatorJump()` when the press is a scroll step, and the wall's scroll-mode arrows call `lineStep` directly, so `readingJumpSerial` never advances mid-walk. The type stays at the size the budget solved, and space is filled with the next paragraph's words rather than by growing the type.

