The toast is fixed — `pieceAtChapterOffset` existed only as a call.

## What was wrong

`applyScrollMode()` maps the page the reader is on back to the slide holding that character when Scroll is turned off, and it did that with `root.pieceAtChapterOffset(...)`. That function was never defined, so every use of the switch threw `TypeError: Property 'pieceAtChapterOffset' of object TheTablePane… is not a function` and the Scroll→Slide restore never ran.

## The fix

I added `pieceAtChapterOffset(charOffset)` next to the page builder in [ReferencePane.qml](qml/components/ReferencePane.qml). It walks the *same* passage text `readingPagesFromChapter` builds — whole items in order, one joining space between them, continuation rows skipped — so a page's `start` means the same thing in both, then refines the part inside the item: for a paragraph split by "Divide long paragraphs" it picks the last row whose text starts at or before the offset, which is the part actually on screen. Turning Scroll off now stages that slide again instead of throwing and leaving the tile on the passage.

As you asked, the split list feature itself is untouched — the list still shows `1-1`, `1-2`, the option still works, and nothing about it was conditioned beyond the passage builder skipping continuations.

## Checks

`tools/dev_cycle.sh --build-only` → `build OK`, `DEV_CYCLE: PASS`, exit 0 (background run, so the tool timeout can't cut it). Relaunched the new build: pid 8480, health OK, and the boot log shows no QML, `ReferenceError`, `is not a function` or `of undefined` lines.

