The Bible path was blocked by the per-tab memory I added, not by the passage builder.

## Why it worked for The Table and not Scripture

The mode is remembered per tab, and those keys were `Bool` with a default of `false`. So when the arrows changed owner from The Table (Scroll on) to Scripture, the wall "adopted" Scripture's stored value — `false` — and quietly flipped the mode back to Slide. Nothing was wrong with the Bible's reading stream; it was never fed because the mode had already been turned off by the adopt step. Table only worked because you had switched it there explicitly.

## Fix

`"never chosen"` is now a distinct state. In [AppSettings.cpp](src/PresentationEngine/modules/settings/AppSettings.cpp) the three keys are `Text` (`""` | `"scroll"` | `"slide"`) instead of `Bool`, and [MonitorWall.qml](qml/components/MonitorWall.qml) adopts a tab's value **only when that tab has one**:

- a tab the operator has never switched **inherits the mode in force** — Scroll on in The Table stays on when you click a verse in Scripture;
- a tab that *has* been switched comes back to its own choice on the next launch, which is the per-tab memory you asked for earlier;
- the switch writes `"scroll"` / `"slide"` for the owning tab only.

Everything downstream was already generic: `ensureScrollSource()` admits `scripture`, and `readingPagesFromChapter()` builds the passage from the pane's own chapter text, so Genesis 1 now starts at the clicked verse and fills with verse 2, 3… by the Scripture tab's own max-words value.

## Checks

`tools/dev_cycle.sh --build-only` → `build OK`, `DEV_CYCLE: PASS`, exit 0. Relaunched: pid 13680, health OK, no QML/JS errors and no "no setting" toast in this boot.

One side effect to know about: your settings store may still hold `table.scrollWalk` as `true`/`false` from the previous build. Those old boolean values are ignored now (the key expects a word), so The Table starts by inheriting whatever mode is current until you press the switch there once.
