Audited the whole QML surface for the same two bug shapes; the DropdownPanel one was the only real arity break, and nothing else calls a function that doesn't exist.

## How I checked

Two scans over `qml/` (skipping `build*`), run as throwaway scripts in `%TEMP%`, not added to the repo:

1. **Signal/handler arity** — collected every `signal name(...)` declared in QML (146 of them) plus every `void name(...)` in headers that declare `signals:` / `Q_SIGNAL` (385 C++ signals), then compared each `onName(a, b)` handler's parameter list against what the signal actually sends. Qt built-ins (`DropArea.entered`, `TapHandler.tapped`, …) are excluded, since their signatures aren't in this source.
2. **Calls to non-existent functions** — collected the functions, callable `property var` bindings, ids and signals each file declares, then flagged every `root.<name>(…)` whose name is declared nowhere in that file (the shape behind `setReadingWordBudget(…)` and `pieceAtChapterOffset(…)`).

## Findings

- **One arity mismatch remains, and it was harmless:** `VGRPresenterMainScreen.qml:2559` had `onResetRequested: (v) => …` on a `SplitHandle` whose `resetRequested()` sends nothing. `v` was always `undefined`, but the body never read it. Removed the parameter so it can't mislead later.
- **Everything else in that class is clean.** After the DropdownPanel fix, both scans report **0 findings**. The five Edit-screen menus that read `item.key` (Auto size, font family, weight, border style and the rest) now receive the row.
- **The root-call scan's only hits were Qt's own methods** — `close()`, `open()`, `requestActivate()`, `startSystemMove()`, `mapToItem()`, `mapFromItem()` on Windows/Items — plus one comment that merely read like a call. Nothing was actually broken.

## Limits worth knowing

The arity scan only sees handlers that *declare* the parameter; a handler that reads an undeclared name (e.g. `onItemActivated: (label) => item.key`) can't be caught without type inference. Signals coming from C++ are only matched when their header declares them in a `signals:` block, and Qt Quick's own item signals are skipped entirely. So this is thorough on the two shapes we actually hit, not a type checker.

