UI is written. Here's what's in the tree now.

**New screen: [RoutingScreen.qml](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml)** — the four-layer canvas, reachable from Settings → **Routing** (nav rail entry + the menu bar's Settings flyout). It is the Audio & Video board's visual language, rebuilt as a node canvas:

- **Buses · Screens · Styles · Outputs** columns over a checkerboard ground, each column headed by the same bordered title pill as the reference, cards on a strict 64px grid so the columns read as one board.
- **Typed wires**: violet = audio, orange = video, blue dashed = a style link. A bus that carries both is wired as two parts — dropping it on NDI makes two parallel wires (each kind leaves and lands 3px off centre so the pair is visible), while dropping it on Speakers makes only the audio half.
- **Rules, in one function** (`wireKinds`): bus→screen is video only, bus→sink is the intersection of the bus's signals and the sink's accepted types, screen→style is a style link, screen→sink is video only. Everything else is refused by dimming the target card to 32% *while the drag is live* rather than silently doing nothing on release.
- **Both drag directions work** (out→in and in→out); the pair is normalised left-to-right because the column order *is* the signal path. Dragging the same pair again removes the wires; right-click removes a single wire (one hit-test layer, sampled along the bezier — never a handler on a wire delegate, which is the crash the Audio & Video board's header records).
- **"Not wired, not sent" is visible**: an unfed screen reads **No signal** in the warning tone, an unrouted bus reads **unrouted**, an output shows exactly what it is receiving (**audio**, **video**, **audio + video**) or **silent**, and a style shows how many screens carry it or **unused**.
- Hovering a card lifts its wires and dims the rest; hovering a wire thickens it; the cursor turns into a pointer over a wire.
- **Add bus** tile closes the buses column (the design says buses are created on this canvas) — it mints "New Bus N" carrying both signals, type editable on Audio & Video after.
