Done — no build, as you asked. Two changes in this pass, on top of the persistence work.

**Styles now trail the board.** Column order is **Buses | Screens | Outputs | Styles**. A style is something a screen *wears*, not a hop a signal makes — sitting between Screens and Outputs it broke up the one connection that is a signal and the wires crossing it read as clutter. The style links now trail off to the right, behind the output cards, answering one question: what does each screen look like. The empty-state notes, headers and hover/drop rules all follow the new order.

**Every card is split by signal.** A bus that carries both now has **two outgoing dots** (video above the centre line, audio below); an output that takes both has **two incoming dots** on the same two lines. Three things fall out of that:

- The drag is typed **from its first pixel** — a gesture that starts on an audio dot can only land on another audio dot, so a video-into-an-audio mistake is structurally impossible rather than refused after the fact. The ghost wire is drawn in the signal's colour and dims while it hovers nothing it can land on.
- A both-bus into a both-output is now **two parallel wires with four distinct endpoints** instead of one ambiguous curve, and each half cuts independently (right-click removes just that side). "The video arrives but the audio does not" is now visible: the dots fill in per signal.
- Output status reads the halves: `both in`, `video only`, `audio only`, `silent` — the old single badge could only ever say one thing about two signals.

A screen keeps a video in-dot and a style out-dot (it has no audio path — the engine's `OutputCapabilities` says so, and the canvas doesn't offer a dot it cannot honour). The system output card has no dots at all, because the mixer sums every audio bus into the default endpoint — a wire there would be a promise nothing keeps.

Also fixed from your screenshot: `1 screen` instead of `1 screens`, and rows with no resolution (an NDI sender, a stream) no longer print a dangling `NDI · `.

**Persistence (the earlier request) is what the wires now write through.** [RoutingService](qml/services/include/services/RoutingService.h) mirrors every roster row as a graph output node `out:<stable id>` and reads/writes that node's `OutputConfig`: a bus wire is `AssignOutputBuses` for **that signal only** (the other half untouched), a style wire is the row's `styleId`. Assignments persist with the production document, are validated by the graph (a bus that isn't a bus of that signal type, audio into a video-only output), and a deleted bus's dangling assignment is cut on the next sync. Engine reads/writes run on the engine worker; the canvas reads a published snapshot, so no binding touches the graph. Rows got a real stable id in [OutputListModel.h](qml/models/include/modules/OutputListModel.h) (healed for older rosters too) so a rename can't orphan a wire, and the session-only `RoutingDraft` is deleted.

**Checks:** `qmllint` on [RoutingScreen.qml](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml), [RoutingPort.qml](qml/components/RoutingPort.qml) and [RoutingNodeCard.qml](qml/components/RoutingNodeCard.qml) — clean. It caught one real error (`opacity` set on a `ShapePath`, which has no such property), fixed by moving it to the `Shape`. The C++ is **not compiled**: this shell can't run `g++` at all (a one-line file exits 1 silently, which is why my earlier attempts looked like empty failures), and `cmake`/`ninja` aren't on this PATH. When you next build, expect a CMake configure first — `RoutingService.cpp/.h` and the `RoutingDraft` removal are in [CMakeLists.txt](CMakeLists.txt).
