{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p6-bb071730","text":"Audio metering requires a TAP hold (startInputMeter per device label) — the Routing page would have to hold taps for its buses' routed sources. There's also a bus mix meter? Let's check how AudioVideoScreen computes "a bus's mix" (comment above says "the blank... a bus's mix"). Search for busMix or busLevel of live.

Also for video: `LiveOutputService.inputDelivery(label, kind)` / `inputProducing(label)` gives flowing/stalled per source label, and outputRuntime[name].frames for outputs.

So the strips:
- Audio half of a bus: the loudest level across its routed audio inputs (needs the metering taps held by whatever page is showing them; the Routing page would have to take holds to show live numbers). That's real work but feasible: hold taps for the routed audio labels of the buses on screen (like AudioVideoScreen).
- Video half of a bus: no level — but "flowing" can be indicated by the first routed video source's delivery state. A strip could paint a thin bar that animates only when frames arrive (indeterminate pulse), or use `outputRuntime` frame counts for outputs.

Hmm, "little level strips under its video and audio dots, so you can see which signal is actually flowing". Perhaps simpler and honest: each wired half gets a tiny 3-state strip:
- audio: a real level bar (needs meter taps) — OR a "flowing/stalled" pulse. Audio only flows if the audio engine exists (not built!). Engine's audio mixing "not built yet" per earlier notes — so audio levels for a BUS mix don't exist; only per-source device meters exist.

Given the engine reality (audio bus mixing not implemented), a bus's audio level can't be true. The honest implementation:
- Audio half: if the bus has routed audio inputs that are delivering, a small bar that reflects the loudest input's meter (requires taps) — we can hold taps for exactly those labels while the Routing page is visible, same refcounted pattern as A&V. That's true and useful.
- Video half: a strip whose fill is the "flowing" state (frames present recently) — animate a marching bar; or reuse `inputDelivery` to decide flowing/stalled/absent and draw the bar in the matching colour.

For outputs: audio half level — an output's audio comes from a bus; if we have bus audio levels we can show the bus feeding it. Video half — `LiveOutputService.outputRuntime[name].frames` / `presented` / `outputFeedStalled(name)`.

Realistic scope: build one component `RoutingLevelStrip.qml` that draws a tiny track with a fill, plus a "flowing" indeterminate mode, and per-card logic deciding what to read:

- Bus video half: source label of first routed video source → `LiveOutputService.inputProducing(label)` → flowing or not. Fill: pulsing bar when flowing; empty track when stalled.
- Bus audio half: loudest among routed audio inputs via a meter snapshot held by the page (labels from AudioInputListModel.getInput(r).sublabel), using EngineBridge.inputLevels; needs the page to hold taps.
- Screen/output video half: for outputs, `outputRuntime[name]` frames + `outputFeedStalled(name)`; for screens (HDMI) also outputRuntime by name.
