Each **wired** half of a bus, screen and output now carries its own little strip under its dot on the Routing canvas.

## What the strip means per half

A strip is drawn only where a wire exists — no wire, no strip — and it sits in the gutter right under the dot the wire leaves from or lands on:

- **Bus · video** (dot on the card's right edge, strip to its right): full bar in the video wire's colour while the bus's first routed source is *delivering* (`LiveOutputService.inputDelivery(...) === "connected"`), empty when it stops or is offline. A picture has no level, so a full/empty bar is the honest reading — an animated fake level would be a lie.
- **Bus · audio**: a real meter. It reads the **loudest routed audio input** through the bridge's tap, not a bus mix — the engine has no audio mixer yet, so the sources are the only truth available, and the comment in the code says exactly that so nobody later mistakes it for a mix meter.
- **Screen / output · video**: the sink's *own* runtime — full only while `outputRuntime[name].presented` and `outputFeedStalled(name)` is false, i.e. the same measured fact its Settings · Screens row prints.
- **Screen / output · audio**: the audio bus wired into it, so a sink shows silence the moment its audio half is unwired rather than a bar borrowed from elsewhere.

An HDMI screen never gets an audio strip (it accepts video only), and the system output — which has no ports and no wire to draw — gets none either: strips mark **wires**, and the mixer's implicit endpoint isn't one.

## Meter taps

Real audio levels need a hold, so the page takes one — with the same discipline Audio & Video uses: `meterTargets` is exactly the labels of audio sources routed into a bus that is itself wired onward (no tap for a meter nobody can see), `syncMeters()` is an idempotent diff (release what left, start what arrived, leave the rest running), and `Component.onDestruction` releases **only this page's own labels**. Never `stopAllInputMeters` — that is the bug that used to kill the Media pane's meters when Settings closed.

## Shared pieces, not new ones

- [LevelTrack.qml](qml/components/LevelTrack.qml) gained one property, `showDot` (default `true`, so every existing meter is untouched) and the bar now takes the full width when that dot is hidden. The canvas already draws the port dot, so these strips are bar-only, drawn from the same component that meters the Audio & Video board.
- Strips are declared **before** the ports Repeater, so the dots and the wires stay on top of them, and they're plain items — they can't eat a connect drag or a wire right-click.

## Verification

`qmllint` on [RoutingScreen.qml](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml) and [LevelTrack.qml](qml/components/LevelTrack.qml): zero syntax errors (only the usual "module not importable standalone" notices). I did not build or launch, per the project rule that the app is yours to build — so the strip geometry against the real cards is unverified on screen.

