**Engine side**
- `OutputStyleSpec` gained a `videoBus` field ([PresentationTypes.hpp](src/PresentationEngine/modules/include/modules/presentation/PresentationTypes.hpp)) — the bus number a row's video wire names, `0` = unwired. The spec was already the per-output channel the live loop reads every frame, so the wire rides it instead of a second push API.
- New `SceneBuilder::BuildBusFeedScene(busId, engine, size)` ([SceneBuilder.hpp](src/PresentationEngine/modules/include/modules/presentation/SceneBuilder.hpp), [SceneBuilder.cpp](src/PresentationEngine/modules/presentation/SceneBuilder.cpp)): one full-frame video layer of that bus's current picture, read from `CompositorState::GetBusFrame` — the *same* frames a design's bus block and a Media › Inputs bus window draw, so a wired "Camera" output shows exactly what the camera's window shows. Scene id carries bus + size so two outputs never share one scene and a resolution edit re-keys; the frame refresh uses the existing seq-skip so a slow camera isn't re-copied every tick; a quiet bus yields an empty frame instead of a frozen picture.
- `LiveOutputController::RenderPerOutputPasses` now composes the bus feed for a wired spec and the gated slide scene for an unwired one (unchanged behaviour). Slide filters are skipped for a bus feed — a slide blur has no business tinting a camera wired straight to a screen.

**UI side**
- `OutputListModel` gained `routedVideoBuses_` + `setRoutedVideoBuses()` (filled by `RoutingService` from the engine's own `OutputConfig`, never a QML copy), and one rule — `hasOwnPass(row)`: *styled or wired* gets its own render pass. `FrameBufferRole`, the live style-set push, `LiveOutputService`'s feed probe and the NDI buffer plan all read that one rule now, so a wired row can't render its bus into a buffer while the rest of the app watches the program feed.
- `RoutingService::publishRoutedVideoBuses()` publishes the mapping after every refresh and immediately after an assignment, so a dropped wire lands within a frame.
- `LiveOutputService::refreshBusFeeds()` now also pulls a feed for every bus a **wire** names, not just buses a design block draws — otherwise a wire into a screen whose slide has no bus block would draw its line and show black. It re-evaluates on the existing 10 Hz mirror, so a re-route settles in ~100 ms.

**Verification:** I typechecked all five changed translation units (`SceneBuilder.cpp`, `LiveOutputController.cpp`, `OutputListModel.cpp`, `RoutingService.cpp`, `LiveOutputService.cpp`) using the project's own flags from `build/compile_commands.json` with `-fsyntax-only` — all `SYNTAX_OK`. Per your "don't build", I did not run the full build, unit tests, or exercise it on screen, so this is compile-clean, not yet behaviour-proven.

Two honest limits to know about: the pass only runs while a presentation is **Live** (`RenderOnce` returns early otherwise), so a camera wired to a screen with no show on air still shows nothing yet; and style→screen remains a style id in `OutputStore` rather than a graph edge. The canvas `+` flows from the earlier message are on disk too — buses open the real bus picker (create mode), styles open the template picker, screens open the Screens page's Add dialog, and Outputs keeps its own transport picker.
