Yes — for the two paths that actually make sound, the audio engine is wired to the canvas. There is one honest gap at the transport ends.

**What is live**

Wiring a source (mic/line) into a bus is not just stored — `AudioEngine::Reconcile()` reads the published graph every pass, and only opens a capture device for a source whose edge lands on an **enabled audio bus** ([AudioEngine.cpp:241-270](src/PresentationEngine/modules/audio/AudioEngine.cpp#L241-L270)). Cut that wire on Settings · Routing and the device closes on the next reconcile; draw it again and it opens. That is a real engine↔UI link, not a mirror.

The program mix is the same chain: buses sum in the mixer, every audio bus is connected to the speakers output when that node is created ([AudioEngine.cpp:164-174](src/PresentationEngine/modules/audio/AudioEngine.cpp#L164-L174)), and the L/R pills under the speaker dot read the same gains and levels (`BusListModel.channelGain`, `ProgramLevels`) that the mixer is rendering — so what those pills show is what the app is playing.

**What is wired but not yet audible**

An audio wire into `out:ndi`, `out:rec` or `out:stream` is accepted, validated and persisted (the graph tags a node's `audioBusId`, and [RecordingService.cpp:292](qml/services/RecordingService.cpp#L292) refuses a take whose node isn't an audio sink). But the mixer renders **one** program mix to the speakers device — there is no per-output audio render feeding those three taps yet. And nothing in the UI calls `startRecordingOn(node, settings)` at all: only the Recording page's generic Start exists, so a take can't yet be aimed at the node you wired on the canvas.

So: bus→speakers audio is genuinely connected end to end; bus→NDI/REC/STREAM audio is connected in the routing model and verified, waiting on a per-tap audio render plus a "record this output" action on those cards.

The last build is green (`DEV_CYCLE: PASS`), including all three guards.

If you want the gap closed, the smallest useful slice is a per-output audio tap in the mixer for the wired sinks plus a Start/Stop action on the REC and STREAM cards, so the canvas itself can begin a take against the node you drew.
