Done — the engine now actually sends a display output's audio somewhere audible.

**How the path works**

The platform renders exactly one playout stream ([IAudio::StartRender](src/PresentationEngine/platform/include/platform/IAudio.hpp#L268) — "one stream at a time, the engine mixes everything into it"), so a monitor can't be a second render client. The fix works with that instead of fighting it: a display output's buses are summed into the program the engine already plays.

- [AudioMixer.cpp:573](src/PresentationEngine/modules/production/AudioMixer.cpp#L573) — after the speaker endpoint is summed, the mixer walks the bus planes and adds every bus that reaches an output flagged `playsLocally` and *not* the speakers. So audio wired to HDMI is in the stream the operator hears; on a booth machine that monitor **is** the render endpoint.
- **Counted once, not twice.** A bus wired to both Speakers and the display is already in the program, so it isn't added again — otherwise simply attaching a monitor would make the program louder. That's why the rule is per-bus rather than "sum each display output's mix".
- **NDI / recording / streaming stay out.** They're deliberately left unflagged: their audio leaves through their own `AddMixTap` sink (the sender or the encoder), and summing them would play a copy of every feed on the speakers nobody wired it to.
- [RoutingService.cpp:531](qml/services/RoutingService.cpp#L531) — the routing sync stamps the flag on each node (`playsLocally` = `"1"` for a display transport, `"0"` otherwise). It's node meta because the mixer reads the graph snapshot, which carries meta and not the output config; it's re-applied on every sync, so your existing `out:out-1` picks it up without a migration.

**Tests** — [tests_production.cpp](src/PresentationEngine/tests/unit/tests_production.cpp#L868), registered as `production / display-output-audio` in [main.cpp](src/PresentationEngine/tests/unit/main.cpp#L240): a bus wired only to the display reaches the program (the wire that used to be silent); the same bus on speakers *and* display measures the same peak as speakers alone (no doubling); and an output without the flag contributes nothing to the program.

**What I did not do:** I didn't build or run the tests, per your instruction. To check it yourself: `tools/dev_cycle.sh` then `build/bps_unit_tests.exe production` (the new case also runs in the full suite).

One limit worth knowing: this plays the display's audio on the engine's playout device. If the HDMI monitor is **not** Windows' default output device, its sound comes out of the default one instead. Routing it to a specific monitor endpoint needs a second render stream in the PAL plus a device choice per output — that's a separate piece of work.
