I got the engine half of this in place, but I stopped short of wiring a device picker, so I want to be precise about what is and isn't live.

**Implemented (not compiled — you asked me not to build)**

- **PAL: a second render stream per output.** [IAudio.hpp](src/PresentationEngine/platform/include/platform/IAudio.hpp#L283) gains `StartRenderStream(streamId, callback, deviceId)` / `StopRenderStream(streamId)`; platforms without a second client keep returning "unsupported" instead of silently playing elsewhere. [WindowsAudio](src/PresentationEngine/platform/windows/WindowsAudio.cpp#L1449) keeps a map of streams keyed by the caller's id, reuses the existing `RenderThread` unchanged, joins outside the lock, and its destructor now stops every stream (a joinable `std::thread` destructor would `terminate`). The program stream is untouched.
- **Mixer: render one output's mix at that device's rate.** [AudioMixer::RenderOutput](src/PresentationEngine/modules/production/AudioMixer.cpp#L613) builds the same bus planes and sums the buses wired to that output node, master-scaled and ceilinged like the program, at the output device's own sample rate — no resampling needed, because the device's callback asks at its own rate. Program metering is untouched.
- **Re-entrancy.** `Render()` and `RenderOutput()` now serialise on the mixer's mutex, including the per-tap sums, because both rebuild the same plane buffers. Without that, a second WASAPI thread could rebuild the planes under the program's taps.
- **AudioEngine**: `StartOutputAudio(nodeId, deviceId)` / `StopOutputAudio` / `StopAllOutputAudio` / `OutputAudioRunning`, opened on the roster's `waveout:<n>`, idempotent per device and re-opening on a device change. `Stop()` now tears output streams down before `mixer_.Attach(nullptr)`.

**Not wired yet — the honest gap**

Nothing calls `StartOutputAudio` yet, so audio wired to an HDMI output still plays through the program stream exactly as it did after the last change. To finish it, an output row needs an `audioDevice` value (a field on `StoredOutput`/`OutputListModel`, alongside `mediaFit`), a picker in the output form fed by `EngineBridge.audioDevices`, and `RoutingService::refresh()` binding/unbinding when the wire or the device changes. I didn't invent an auto-matching rule (monitor names don't map reliably onto WASAPI endpoint names), because a wrong guess would silently route sound to the wrong speakers.

Two things worth knowing before you build it: with several endpoints the program and each output's callback contend on one mixer mutex (fine for one extra device; separating the plane buffers is the follow-up if you run many), and none of this has been compiled or exercised — `tools/dev_cycle.sh` then `build/bps_unit_tests.exe production` would cover the existing suites, though the new stream path itself needs a real second endpoint to hear.
