Per-output audio is now wired end to end: a row can name an endpoint, and its stream opens and closes with its wires.

**The choice, persisted**

[StoredOutput::audioDevice](src/PresentationEngine/modules/include/modules/project/OutputStore.hpp#L55) holds the audio roster's own id (`waveout:<n>`); empty means the program, so every existing roster round-trips unchanged (absent key, no migration). It survives the store's JSON via the same string path as `mediaFit`.

**The model**

[OutputListModel](qml/models/OutputListModel.cpp#L1112) carries it as `audioDevice` — hydrate, save, `AudioDeviceRole`, and inside `getOutput`, so both settings dialogs read it from one accessor. `setAudioDevice` persists and then re-asks the routing pass, because the device is part of the routing: gaining an endpoint must start playing there, giving one up must go back into the program rather than go silent.

**The binding — wires decide, device says where**

[bindOutputAudio](qml/services/RoutingService.cpp#L795), called on the same engine-worker pass that reads the wired sets, opens a row's stream only when it has an endpoint *and* at least one audio bus wired to it, and stops every other row — a stream on an unwired row would hold a WASAPI client open (a device other apps then can't use). The device id is parsed by [AudioDeviceNumber](qml/services/RoutingService.cpp#L205); an id that no longer matches an attached endpoint opens **nothing** rather than falling back to the default, because silently playing on the wrong speakers defeats the whole choice. One toast per row per distinct failure, not one per refresh.

Two consequences handled alongside: a row that plays on its own endpoint now gets `playsLocally = 0`, so its mix isn't *also* summed into the program (that would be the same sound twice, from two places); and a deleted row stops its stream before its node is pruned.

**The picker**

[Plays audio on](qml/components/ScreenForm.qml#L339) sits under Resolution/Refresh — outward devices only (a microphone is not a destination), with "Same as program" first, and a saved device that is no longer attached reads "*id* (not connected)" instead of pretending to be the program. The list re-reads on `devicesChanged`, and the pick is reported as an id, not a label (two panels can share a name). Because Settings · Outputs' Add/Edit and Settings · Routing's Configure output are the same form, both doors set it ([submitOutput](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L452)).

