Fixed in the canvas's port rule — the dot list was adding both halves for *every* bus, whatever its type said.

**What was wrong**

`ports` did this for each bus row:

```qml
add(key, "video", "out")
add(key, "audio", "out")
```

unconditionally, and `busHasVideo`/`busHasAudio` — the two helpers the strips, pills and geometry already consult — were stubs that returned `true` (`function busHasVideo(index) { void index; return true }`). So "Laptop Mic" (type *audio*) got an orange video dot, even though `BusListModel::connectRefusal` refuses every video route into an audio bus with `audio-only-bus`: the dot was a port nothing could ever land on.

**What changed** ([RoutingScreen.qml](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml))

- [busHasVideo](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L738) now reads the bus roster's own `type`: an `"audio"` bus has no picture, so `"video"` and `"both"` send one. `busHasAudio` stays true for every bus on purpose — a `"video"` bus is a camera/feed bus and the mixer sums its sources' embedded audio (`AudioMixer::FeedsForBus` reads the paired `#v` plane for the `#a` one), so its audio dot is a wire the engine really carries.
- [ports](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L957) adds only the halves a bus carries, so an audio bus gets its audio dot alone.
- [cardHasTwoDots](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L650) follows the same rule for buses — a one-signal bus has a single dot, and a single dot sits on the centreline. That also corrects the Outputs column: it was claiming two dots for every non-speaker output, so a video-only display's lone dot was offset upward; it now keys off whether that output accepts *both* signals.

Consequences worth knowing: an audio bus no longer shows a video strip (that strip was gated by the same stub), and its audio dot moves from the lower slot to the card's centre, with the L/R pair following it — `portOffset`, the pills and the wires all derive from the count, so they move together. `pairOk` already gated on these two helpers, so the canvas now refuses a video drag onto an audio bus for the same reason the model does, instead of drawing a promise it can't keep.

**Checks:** `qmllint` on the file — exit 0, no diagnostics beyond the usual "failed to import VGRPresenterUI" module-path warning that every file gets outside the build. Nothing built or launched, per your instruction.
