Both files lint clean. Here's where the feature actually stands.

## Is it done?

**Yes, as of this turn — with one gap that was still open when I checked, now closed.**

The two halves that make up the request:

**Window level (independent assignment).** A bus item on a design is a window, and each one names its own bus in its meta (`busId`), set from the Edit canvas — select the window, its bus picker is the row under the shape tools ([EditScreen.qml:1434](qml/screens/edit/EditScreen.qml#L1434)). The engine paints each window from that block's own `busId` ([SceneBuilder.cpp:1441](src/PresentationEngine/modules/presentation/SceneBuilder.cpp#L1441)), so three windows can show three different buses, independently.

**Output level (direct vs compose).** Each output row carries `videoMode` (`direct` | `compose`), persisted only when non-default ([OutputStore.cpp:83](src/PresentationEngine/modules/project/OutputStore.cpp#L83)), and it decides two things at once in [OutputListModel.cpp:1495](qml/models/OutputListModel.cpp#L1495):
- `compose` → the design is composed and windows paint their own assigned bus (`gateVideoBuses = false`);
- `direct` → the routed bus fills the frame by itself and the gate closes with an empty allow-list, so no window paints.

The *program* pass never paints a window ([OutputListModel.cpp:1447](qml/models/OutputListModel.cpp#L1447)), which is what stops an unwired output from inheriting the active row's picture. Both modes push immediately on toggle, so the frame changes next tick.

**The gap I found:** the direct/compose switch only existed on sink cards (NDI/recording/stream). An HDMI screen — the main case — had no way to choose. It's now on screen cards too ([RoutingScreen.qml:525](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L525)), same action, same handler.

## The gate chips, as you asked

Overlays and Media are now clickable nodes on every output card instead of menu-only:

- The card component gained `gateChips` / `overlayOn` / `mediaOn` + a `gateToggled` signal ([RoutingNodeCard.qml](qml/components/RoutingNodeCard.qml)). Each chip is **a dot, a short connector stub, then the initial** (`O`, `M`) — the connector vocabulary the canvas already uses — lit in the accent when the gate is open, muted and hollow when closed.
- They sit on the subtitle line, not the title line: sink cards carry a status pill *and* a transport pill there, and the subtitle line already gives up width to the status text, so the chips subtract their width the same way instead of drawing over anything.
- Not ports: they live inside the card, never reach the canvas's port hit-test, and their MouseArea claims the press so neither a double-click edit nor a wire drag starts from them. A press just flips that row's flag ([toggleCardGate](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L1845)).
- Wired on screen cards and on every sink except the system speaker endpoint (that node is the engine's, with no roster row to write).
- The old `MEDIA OFF` / `OVR OFF` text badge is gone — the chips say the same thing, and two ways of saying it on one 56 px card was clutter. The menu entries stay as the keyboard-ish path.

## Checks

`qmllint` clean on both edited files (Qt 6.11 import path), no unused leftovers. I did **not** build or launch, because your running instance holds `appVGRPresenterUI.exe` and `dev_cycle.sh` would kill it — so the chips' look, the click round-trip, and compose mode on an HDMI row are compiled-and-wired but not seen on screen yet. Build + launch is yours.

The compose switch also lives only in the card's menu, alongside the gates in the menu — if you'd rather have a visible "direct/compose" toggle on the card too, that's the same chip mechanism one row further.
