Done — no build run, per your instruction. Here's what the gate cards are now.

## They're wire sources, like a bus

**The card** ([RoutingScreen.qml:2445](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L2445)) sits at the head of the Styles column with its own **out dot** on the column's right edge — one dot, on the centreline, because a permission is one thing. No menu, no clickable body: the dot is the whole control.

**The wire is the permission.** Drag from Overlays → an output and that output may paint the overlay layer; same for Media. Cut the wire (right-click, or drag it again) and the permission goes. The line is drawn dashed in the style colour, landing 6 px either side of the output's style wire (overlay above, media below), so a row wearing a style *and* allowing both layers shows three lines, not one smudge.

**How it's wired inside the canvas** — the gate is a first-class node, not a special case bolted on:
- keys `gate:overlay` / `gate:media`, in column 1 ([columnOf/rowOf](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L762)), positioned at `rowTop` so the style cards still start below them ([cardTopOf](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L298));
- reachable by the drop hit-test ([nodeAt](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L1423)), so a wire can also be pulled *back* from an output's dot onto the card;
- accepted by [pairOk](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L1339) for screens and sinks, refused for the speaker endpoint (no picture to dress);
- produced in the wire list ([wires](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L993)) by reading the rows, so a line can never disagree with what the engine paints;
- toggled/cut in [toggleWire](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L1471) and [removeWireAt](qml/screens/settings/RoutingScreen.qml#L1573), both write the row's flag through `OutputListModel`.

**No wire, nothing paints** — as you chose. The stored default flipped to not-allowed ([OutputStore.hpp:66](src/PresentationEngine/modules/include/modules/project/OutputStore.hpp#L66), [OutputListModel.h:89](qml/models/include/models/OutputListModel.h#L89)); the JSON now writes the flags when *open*, so an unwired roster stays byte-identical. Every gate toggle also re-publishes the routing rows, so the line appears on the same frame you let go of the dot rather than at the next unrelated refresh.

Consequences worth knowing before you look at it:

- **Everything is dark until wired.** Overlays and media stop painting on every output, including the active one and therefore the program preview, until a gate wire reaches that row. That's the allow-list you asked for, but it will read as "the overlay vanished" on first launch.
- The card shows the count (`off`, `1 output`, `3 outputs`), lit only when at least one wire leaves it.
- The `O`/`M` connector chips on output cards still work and write the **same flag** — they're a shortcut for the wire, not a second rule. The style's own gate still applies on top, so a wire opens what the theme offers and never what it forbids.
- I did not build or launch, so nothing here has been seen running. Only `qmllint` was run, clean on both edited QML files.
