A template bus now accepts only what its template can show. The rule lives where routing policy already lives, in [BusListModel.cpp](qml/models/BusListModel.cpp):

**Capacity = windows.** `templateWindows()` reads the bus's template design and counts its `bus` blocks: `total` (all windows) and `open` (those that name no bus — `meta.busId` empty). An *open* window is one the template bus's own routed feeds fill, in route order; a window that names a bus shows that bus, so no feed routed into the template bus can land in it. That gives three refusals, each with its own wording:

- `template-has-no-windows` — the template has no bus items at all, so there is nowhere for the feed to go (your Default case: The Table and Camera no longer connect).
- `template-windows-assigned` — every window names its own bus, so the feeds belong on those buses, not on the template bus.
- `template-windows-full` — every free window already carries a feed.

Enforced twice, on purpose: `connectRefusal()` is what the board's red card, dashed ghost and toast read, and `connectVideoRoute()` refuses the same cases for the doors that skip the board (the bus dialog's toggle list, scripts). The sentence the operator reads is in [AudioVideoScreen.qml](qml/screens/settings/AudioVideoScreen.qml#L1484), and the code list is documented in [BusListModel.h](qml/models/include/models/BusListModel.h#L232).

**Already-accepted routes get corrected.** The existing startup sweep (`trimExtraVideoProviders`) used to skip template buses entirely; it now holds them to their template's free windows, so the two wires in your screenshot are dropped on the next launch instead of lingering as wires that show nothing. That trim is gated on `known` — when the template library hasn't loaded yet the design map is empty for a reason unrelated to the template, and "not read yet" must not be read as "no windows" and sweep valid routes away.

